{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793704519,"nonce":"I8IvQ40MCmvkExkLQS32dtmJtqk=","title":"Follow-up to 5d5fc8a, which refuses `on: cron:` at pipeline check until this exists. Filed 2026-11-03.","message":"Goal: a pipeline with `on: cron:` gets a Run at each scheduled time, on every replica count, with no external CronJob (needed by the public demo keep-alive, now a GitHub Actions force-push, and the dogfood canary 33fc3ab).\n\nWhat exists and can be reused: `dispatch_run` (crates/scarab-server/src/lib.rs) already resolves a ref to a commit, reads one named pipeline there, compiles it, checks the trigger opt-in and admits through the shared path. ADR-0077 gives a leader with term fencing in-transaction. A cron Run should be \"a dispatch the leader makes\", not a new path.\n\nWhat is missing (each is a decision or real work):\n1. Syntax. `TriggerFilter` has only `when:` and `deny_unknown_fields`, so there is nowhere to write a schedule today: `on: cron: {}` is the only accepted form. Decide the shape (`schedule:` string or list, branch, timezone — UTC only?), and how the per-kind field fits the shared `TriggerFilter`.\n2. Discovery. Pipelines are read from the repo only when an event arrives. Nothing stores which repos have cron pipelines, so the leader has nothing to tick over. Options: (a) a schedule index, written when a push to the scheduled branch compiles a pipeline (and when a repo is bound), deleted when the pipeline or trigger goes; (b) poll every bound repo's branch on a timer (forge API cost, rate limits). (a) goes stale if a push webhook is missed; say how it recovers.\n3. Which commit. A cron Run builds the scheduled branch's head at fire time. `ForgePort` has no default-branch method (the GitHub adapter fetches `default_branch` inline only in `create_deployment`); add one, or require the branch in the syntax.\n4. Event shape and governance. `Event::Cron { schedule }` has no repo, ref or commit, and 12 sites match on it. Environment governance treats a cron run as having no symbolic ref, so it is refused once any layer restricts refs (`environment_governance.rs` test \"A cron run has no symbolic ref\"). Giving cron a ref changes what `allowed_refs` admits; decide that on purpose. Also: Actor (none — Policies and `run.create` must handle a machine actor), Headline (ADR-0057 says the schedule expression).\n5. Firing. Leader-only tick; durable last-fired per (repo, pipeline path, schedule); at most once per slot across replicas (a unique row per slot inside the leader's fenced transaction); after downtime fire the latest missed slot once, never N times; a slot whose dispatch is refused (compile error, governance) is recorded as refused, not retried forever.\n6. Dependency. A cron-expression parser crate (external dep; needs a why and a pick).\n7. Tests (classical): slot maths with a fake clock; two replicas fire one Run per slot (PG); missed slots fire once; a schedule removed by a push stops firing; a refused slot is visible as a refused run. Kind tier: one real fire.\n8. Docs: pipeline-syntax.mdx (`on: cron`), remove the \"never fires\" sentence, index.mdx \"Missing\" list.\n\nAcceptance: a pipeline with `on: cron:` on a bound repo gets exactly one Run per slot with two server replicas, the Run shows its schedule as the Headline, and removing the trigger in a push stops it.","files":null}]}